---
// The OpenAI Decisions API as benchmarked: accuracy, confidence, speed and price from the scored runs
// (studies/, answers/), OpenAI's own claims kept apart from our measurements. The older log-probability
// probe of GPT-6 Luna through the chat endpoint (probes/luna-logprobs/analysis.json) follows as a
// labelled secondary section; it is outside the scored benchmark.
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import DepthChart from "../../components/DepthChart.astro";
import Legend from "../../components/Legend.astro";
import AccTable from "../../components/AccTable.astro";
import DiffRows from "../../components/DiffRows.astro";
import EngineName from "../../components/EngineName.astro";
import AnthusNote from "../../components/AnthusNote.astro";
import ExampleCard from "../../components/ExampleCard.astro";
import ModelErrors from "../../components/ModelErrors.astro";
import RequestExample from "../../components/RequestExample.astro";
import { data, modelById, urls, OWA, CWA, taskOrder, TASK_INFO, taskBySlug, pct, pts, fmt, int, overall, axisRow, axisTable, pair, latency, costOf, derived, L, listOf } from "../../lib/site.js";
import { intro, DECISIONS_API } from "../../lib/content.js";

const API = "openai-decisions-gpt-6-luna";
const LUNA = "openai-gpt-6-luna-effort-none";
const S = DECISIONS_API.sources;
const P = data.probe;
const ids = ["jev", API, LUNA];
const pt = (s) => P && P.tasks[s];
const top = (s, engine = null) => { const t = pt(s); const b = engine ? (t.other_engines_same_items[engine] || {}).bands : t.bands; return b ? b.find((x) => x.from >= 0.99) : null; };
const others = (s) => Object.keys(pt(s).other_engines_same_items);
const ENGINE_LABEL = (e) => (modelById[e] ? modelById[e].label : e);
const bandLabel = (b) => (b.from >= 0.99 ? "99% or more" : `${pct(b.from, 0)}% to ${pct(b.to, 0)}%`);
const wrongEx = P ? P.examples.filter((e) => !e.correct) : [];
const rightEx = P ? P.examples.filter((e) => e.correct) : [];
const ex = wrongEx.find((e) => e.task === OWA) || wrongEx[0];
const pretty = (o) => JSON.stringify(o, null, 2);
const totalNotArgmax = P ? taskOrder.reduce((a, s) => a + (pt(s) ? pt(s).returned_not_most_probable : 0), 0) : 0;
const totalResponses = P ? taskOrder.reduce((a, s) => a + (pt(s) ? pt(s).responses : 0), 0) : 0;
const masses = P ? taskOrder.map((s) => pt(s).mean_disclosed_mass) : [];
const share = (id, s, gold, said) => { const row = overall(id, s) && overall(id, s).confusion[gold]; if (!row) return null; const n = Object.values(row).reduce((x, y) => x + y, 0); return n ? row[said] / n : null; };
const conf = (s) => pt(s) && pt(s).decisions_api_confidence;
const lat = latency(API, OWA);
const cost = costOf(API);
const apiEx = data.requestExamples && data.requestExamples.tasks[OWA] ? data.requestExamples.tasks[OWA][0] : null;
const dep = (id, v) => axisRow(id, OWA, "depth", v);
---
<Base title={intro.preview.seoTitle} description={intro.preview.intro} path={urls.preview()} current={{ page: "preview" }}
  crumbs={[{ label: "Benchmark", href: urls.home() }, { label: "Models", href: urls.models() }, { label: "OpenAI Decisions API" }]}>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <p class="kicker">Benchmark · OpenAI Decisions API (gpt-6-luna)</p>
    <h1 class="display xl">{intro.preview.title}</h1>
    <p class="deck">{intro.preview.intro}</p>
    {overall(API, OWA) && overall(API, CWA) && (
      <p class="finding"><span class="finding-num">{pct(overall(API, OWA).accuracy)}%</span><span class="finding-txt">The OpenAI Decisions API answered {pct(overall(API, OWA).accuracy)}% of open-world problems and {pct(overall(API, CWA).accuracy)}% of closed-world ones correctly. <EngineName engine={modelById.jev} href={urls.model("jev")} /> answered {pct(overall("jev", OWA).accuracy)}% and {pct(overall("jev", CWA).accuracy)}% of the same problems.</span></p>
    )}
    <p class="footnote">Every model got the same problem, question and option descriptions, one request per problem. We sent the Decisions API the benchmark's own wording and did not test other wordings of the instructions.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" aria-labelledby="what-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="what-h">What the OpenAI Decisions API is</h2>
    <p class="lede">OpenAI made the Decisions API generally available on 22 October 2026 (<a href={S.announcement.url}>{S.announcement.label}</a>). A request goes to <code>POST /v1/decisions</code> with the model <code>gpt-6-luna</code>, and each question is a predicate, a choice or a score. A choice answer returns the chosen option, a probability for every option and a separate confidence (<a href={S.guide.url}>{S.guide.label}</a>).</p>
    <p>OpenAI prices it at $0.10 per million uncached input tokens and does not charge for output tokens, and says it "returns typed answers about 10x faster than the Responses API" (<a href={S.guide.url}>{S.guide.label}</a>). Those are OpenAI's statements; what we measured is below.</p>
    <p>We sent one choice question per problem: the problem text as the input, the question's instructions, and each option with its description. The option set is the task's ({listOf(taskBySlug[OWA].options.map((o) => `"${o}"`))} on the open-world task). GPT-6 Luna through the chat endpoint with reasoning off, which has its own entry on this site, is a different way of reaching the same model name.</p>
  </section>

  {apiEx && apiEx.engines[API] && (
    <section class="wrap section sec-prose" aria-labelledby="req-h">
      <h2 class="display sm" id="req-h">Exactly what it was asked</h2>
      <p class="lede">One open-world problem at proof depth {apiEx.depth}. The correct answer is <strong>{apiEx.gold}</strong>.</p>
      <RequestExample id={API} ex={apiEx} />
      <p class="more-link"><a href={urls.asked()}>Every model's request and response</a></p>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="wrap section sec-split" aria-labelledby="acc-h">
    <div class="sec-head">
    <h2 class="display" id="acc-h">Accuracy by proof depth</h2>
    <p class="lede">The same 1,800 problems per task for every model, one request each.{overall(API, OWA) && overall("jev", OWA) && ` The Decisions API answered ${pct(overall(API, OWA).accuracy)}% of open-world problems correctly, Jev ${pct(overall("jev", OWA).accuracy)}% and GPT-6 Luna through the chat endpoint ${pct(overall(LUNA, OWA).accuracy)}%; on the closed-world task, ${pct(overall(API, CWA).accuracy)}%, ${pct(overall("jev", CWA).accuracy)}% and ${pct(overall(LUNA, CWA).accuracy)}%.`}</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sec-body">
    <div class="two-up">
      {taskOrder.map((s) => (
        <figure class="hero-fig">
          <figcaption><span class="fig-t">{TASK_INFO[s].long}</span></figcaption>
          <Legend ids={ids} slug={s} />
          <DepthChart slug={s} ids={ids} compact label={`Jev, the OpenAI Decisions API and GPT-6 Luna by proof depth, ${TASK_INFO[s].short}`} />
        </figure>
      ))}
    </div>
    {dep(API, "0") && dep(API, "5") && <p>On the open-world task it scored {pct(dep(API, "0").accuracy, 0)}% when the answer is stated outright (depth 0) and {pct(dep(API, "5").accuracy, 0)}% at five chained inferences; Jev scored {pct(dep("jev", "0").accuracy, 0)}% and {pct(dep("jev", "5").accuracy, 0)}%.</p>}
    {taskOrder.map((s) => { const p = pair(s, "jev", API); return p && (
      <Fragment>
        <h3>Jev minus the OpenAI Decisions API by depth, {TASK_INFO[s].short.toLowerCase().replace(" ", "-")} task</h3>
        <DiffRows rows={p.filter((r) => r.axis === "depth")} labelOf={(r) => `depth ${r.value}`} label={`Jev minus the OpenAI Decisions API by depth, ${TASK_INFO[s].short}`} />
      </Fragment>
    ); })}
    <h3>Accuracy by depth, open-world task</h3>
    <AccTable rows={axisTable(OWA, "depth", ids)} ids={ids} slug={OWA} valueHead="Proof depth" caption="By depth, open world" />
    <p class="more-link"><a href={urls.comparison("jev-vs-openai-decisions-api")}>Jev vs the OpenAI Decisions API in full</a>: every breakdown, answer class and paired difference.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-split" aria-labelledby="err-h">
    <div class="sec-head">
    <h2 class="display" id="err-h">Which answers it gets wrong</h2>
    <p class="lede">Rows are the correct answer; columns are what the model said.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sec-body">
    <p>On the open-world task it answered unknown for {pct(share(API, OWA, "true", "unknown"), 0)}% of problems whose correct answer is true and {pct(share(API, OWA, "false", "unknown"), 0)}% of those whose answer is false; Jev did so for {pct(share("jev", OWA, "true", "unknown"), 0)}% and {pct(share("jev", OWA, "false", "unknown"), 0)}%. On the closed-world task it answered true for {pct(share(API, CWA, "false", "true"), 0)}% of problems whose answer is false, against {pct(share(LUNA, CWA, "false", "true"), 0)}% for GPT-6 Luna through the chat endpoint and {pct(share("jev", CWA, "false", "true"), 0)}% for Jev.</p>
    <ModelErrors id={API} named />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" aria-labelledby="conf-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="conf-h">Its confidence</h2>
    <p class="lede">A decision model is most useful when it also says how sure it is, so you can act on confident answers and send the rest to a person. The Decisions API returns a probability for each option and a separate confidence, and the scored run recorded both.</p>
    {conf(OWA) && conf(CWA) && <p>We checked whether the confidence separates right answers from wrong ones (AUROC, where 0.5 is no better than chance and 1 is perfect). It scored <strong>{fmt(conf(OWA).auroc, 3)}</strong> on the open-world task and <strong>{fmt(conf(CWA).auroc, 3)}</strong> on the closed-world task{pt(OWA).other_engines_same_items.jev && pt(CWA).other_engines_same_items.jev ? `; the probability Jev gave its own answer scored ${fmt(pt(OWA).other_engines_same_items.jev.auroc, 3)} and ${fmt(pt(CWA).other_engines_same_items.jev.auroc, 3)} on the same items` : ""}. The probabilities are rounded to two decimals, so the confidence took only {int(conf(OWA).distinct_values)} distinct values on the open-world task and {int(conf(CWA).distinct_values)} on the closed-world one.</p>}
    {taskOrder.some((s) => derived(API, s) && derived(API, s).calibration) && <p>{taskOrder.filter((s) => derived(API, s) && derived(API, s).calibration).map((s) => `On the ${TASK_INFO[s].short.toLowerCase()} task its probability for its own answer averaged ${pct(derived(API, s).calibration.mean_confidence)}% against ${pct(derived(API, s).calibration.accuracy)}% accuracy (expected calibration error ${pct(derived(API, s).calibration.ece)} points).`).join(" ")}</p>}
    <p>Any recalibration that keeps the order of the scores leaves AUROC unchanged, so where AUROC is near 0.5 no remapping of the number can pick out which answers to trust.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" aria-labelledby="spd-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="spd-h">Speed and price</h2>
    {lat ? <p>OpenAI's claim of a roughly tenfold speed-up over the Responses API is OpenAI's; we did not time the Responses API. What we measured is the Decisions API's own time per request: a median of {int(Math.round(lat.p50))} ms and a 90th percentile of {int(Math.round(lat.p90))} ms over the network, from the scored run with {lat.concurrency} requests at a time. These times include the network and OpenAI's servers, so they are not like for like with models that ran on a laptop. <a href={urls.speed()}>Speed for every model</a>.</p>
      : <p>OpenAI's claim of a roughly tenfold speed-up over the Responses API is OpenAI's; we did not time it.</p>}
    {cost && <p>At the list price of $0.10 per million input tokens, with output tokens not charged, the {int(cost.total.requests)} requests used {int(cost.total.input_tokens)} input tokens and cost ${fmt(cost.total.usd, 2)} in all: ${fmt(cost.total.usd_per_million, 0)} per million decisions and ${fmt(cost.total.usd_per_million_correct, 0)} per million correct decisions. <a href={urls.cost()}>Cost per decision for every hosted model</a>.</p>}
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" aria-labelledby="two-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="two-h">The same model name through two endpoints</h2>
    <p>GPT-6 Luna through OpenAI's chat endpoint with reasoning off scored {pct(overall(LUNA, OWA).accuracy)}% and {pct(overall(LUNA, CWA).accuracy)}%, and the Decisions API, which also serves gpt-6-luna, scored {pct(overall(API, OWA).accuracy)}% and {pct(overall(API, CWA).accuracy)}%: {fmt(100 * (overall(LUNA, OWA).accuracy - overall(API, OWA).accuracy), 1)} and {fmt(100 * (overall(LUNA, CWA).accuracy - overall(API, CWA).accuracy), 1)} points lower on the same problems. We do not know why. The depth-0 results ({pct(dep(API, "0").accuracy, 0)}% open world) show the request and scoring work as intended. We did not test other wordings of the instructions.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" aria-labelledby="lim-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="lim-h">Limits</h2>
    <ul class="how-list">
      <li>One dataset, and a synthetic one: ProofWriter's templated reasoning problems, not your decisions.</li>
      <li>One wording of the instructions and option descriptions, the same for every model. Other wordings may score differently.</li>
      <li>Each problem was sent once, so repeatability of the Decisions API is not measured.</li>
      <li>Times are from a run with {lat ? lat.concurrency : "several"} requests at a time and include the network.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" id="chat-probe" aria-labelledby="probe-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="probe-h">Secondary: an earlier probe of GPT-6 Luna's log-probabilities on the chat endpoint</h2>
    <p class="lede">Before the Decisions API was documented we asked whether GPT-6 Luna through the chat endpoint, with reasoning off, could supply a confidence by returning log-probabilities. That probe is separate from the scored benchmark and from the API results above; it changes no number elsewhere on this site.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" aria-labelledby="lp-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="lp-h">Can chat-endpoint Luna give you a confidence?</h2>
    <p class="lede">A decision model is most useful when it also says how sure it is, so you can act on confident answers and send the rest to a person. The scored benchmark asked every model for its answer only. To see whether GPT-6 Luna could supply a confidence, we read its log-probabilities.</p>
    <p>A language model writes its reply one token (a word or piece of a word) at a time, and at each step it assigns a probability to every possible next token. A log-probability is the natural logarithm of that probability: 0 means certain, and the more negative it is, the less likely. With <code>logprobs</code> turned on, the API returns the log-probability of each token it wrote, and with <code>top_logprobs</code> the most likely alternatives at each step.</p>
    <p>Our requests used a strict JSON schema whose only allowed values are the task's options, so every reply is a small object such as <code>{`{"answer":"false"}`}</code>. We find the tokens that spell the answer inside that reply, add their log-probabilities and convert the sum back to a probability: that is the probability Luna gave its own answer. The options begin differently ({listOf(taskBySlug[OWA].options.map((o) => `"${o}"`))}), so the first answer token already identifies one option, and each alternative Luna lists at that position maps to exactly one other option.</p>
    {P && <p>With reasoning off, Luna exposed these probabilities almost completely. On most responses not every option appears among the alternatives, but the options it lists carry {listOf(masses.map((m) => `${pct(m)}%`))} of the probability on average (open world, closed world), so the ones left out carry almost none. With reasoning on, the API refuses the parameter: "'logprobs' is not supported with this model" (<a href={S.guide.url}>{S.guide.label}</a>).</p>}
  </section>

  {ex && (
    <section class="wrap section sec-split" aria-labelledby="ex-h">
      <div class="sec-head">
      <h2 class="display" id="ex-h">A real request and response, verbatim</h2>
      <p class="lede">One of the probe's 3,600 requests, exactly as sent and as returned: a {TASK_INFO[ex.task].short.toLowerCase().replace(" ", "-")} problem at proof depth {ex.depth} ({ex.id}). The correct answer is <strong>{ex.gold}</strong>. Luna answered <strong>{ex.answer}</strong>, and the log-probability of that answer's token, {fmt(Math.log(ex.stated), 4)}, is a probability of <strong>{pct(ex.stated, 2)}%</strong>.{ex.alternatives.length > 1 ? ` The alternatives it listed: ${ex.alternatives.map((a) => `"${a.token}" ${pct(a.p, 2)}%`).join(", ")}.` : ` It listed no other option at that position.`}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sec-body">
      <p>That is a confidently wrong answer: stated as close to certain, and wrong. On its own it proves nothing; the results below show how often it happens.</p>
      <h3>The request body</h3>
      <pre class="code verbatim"><code>{pretty(ex.request)}</code></pre>
      <h3>The response</h3>
      <pre class="code verbatim"><code>{pretty(ex.response)}</code></pre>
      {rightEx.length > 0 && (
        <details class="cell">
          <summary>A right answer, for comparison ({rightEx[0].id}, stated {pct(rightEx[0].stated, 2)}%, correct answer {rightEx[0].gold})</summary>
          <pre class="code verbatim"><code>{pretty(rightEx[0].response)}</code></pre>
        </details>
      )}
      <p class="note">Every request and response is kept verbatim in <code>probes/luna-logprobs/</code>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" aria-labelledby="spot-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="spot-h">The spot check that started it</h2>
    <p class="lede">We first sent a dozen deep open-world problems to GPT-6 Luna with log-probabilities turned on. With reasoning off, Luna returned the chosen answer's log-probability and at most one or two alternatives, and gave 5 of the 12 wrong answers at 97.8% or more.</p>
    <p>Twelve items prove nothing, so we ran a full probe: every one of the {int(totalResponses)} benchmark items, sent with exactly the benchmark's GPT-6 Luna request plus <code>logprobs</code> and <code>top_logprobs: 5</code>. It is outside the scored benchmark and changes no benchmark number.</p>
  </section>

  {P && (
    <section class="wrap section sec-split" aria-labelledby="res-h">
      <div class="sec-head">
      <h2 class="display" id="res-h">Results: when Luna says 99%, how often is it right?</h2>
      <p class="lede">Each answer is grouped by the probability the model gave it. A well-calibrated model is right about as often as it says. Jev's probabilities are its own, from the scored run, on the same items.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sec-body">
      {taskOrder.map((s) => { const t = pt(s); const j = t.other_engines_same_items.jev; return (
        <Fragment>
          <h3>{TASK_INFO[s].long}</h3>
          <div class="table-scroll">
            <table class="cell-table">
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Stated probability</th><th scope="col" class="num">Luna answers</th><th scope="col" class="num">Luna stated, average</th><th scope="col" class="num">Luna right</th>{j && <Fragment><th scope="col" class="num">Jev answers</th><th scope="col" class="num">Jev right</th></Fragment>}</tr></thead>
              <tbody>{t.bands.map((b, i) => { const jb = j && j.bands[i]; return (
                <tr><th scope="row">{bandLabel(b)}</th><td class="num">{int(b.n)}</td><td class="num">{b.n ? `${pct(b.mean_stated)}%` : "—"}</td><td class="num strong">{b.n ? `${pct(b.accuracy)}%` : "—"}</td>
                  {j && <Fragment><td class="num">{int(jb.n)}</td><td class="num">{jb.n ? `${pct(jb.accuracy)}%` : "—"}</td></Fragment>}</tr>
              ); })}</tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </Fragment>
      ); })}

      <h3>Every model, same items</h3>
      <p>Expected calibration error (ECE) is the average gap between stated probability and accuracy; 0 is perfect. "Wrong at 95%" is the share of wrong answers stated at 95% or more. AUROC is how well the stated probability separates right answers from wrong ones: 1 is perfect and 0.5 is no better than a coin flip.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="cell-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">Task</th><th scope="col" class="num">Accuracy, this run</th><th scope="col" class="num">ECE</th><th scope="col" class="num">Wrong at 95%+</th><th scope="col" class="num">Wrong at 99%+</th><th scope="col" class="num">AUROC</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {taskOrder.map((s) => { const t = pt(s); return (
              <Fragment>
                <tr><th scope="row"><EngineName engine={modelById[LUNA]} /></th><td>{TASK_INFO[s].short}</td><td class="num">{pct(t.accuracy)}%</td><td class="num strong">{fmt(t.ece, 3)}</td><td class="num">{pct(t.wrong_at_95)}%</td><td class="num">{pct(t.wrong_at_99)}%</td><td class="num strong">{fmt(t.auroc, 3)}</td></tr>
                {others(s).map((e) => { const o = t.other_engines_same_items[e]; return (
                  <tr><th scope="row">{modelById[e] ? <EngineName engine={modelById[e]} /> : e}</th><td>{TASK_INFO[s].short}</td><td class="num">{pct(o.accuracy)}%</td><td class="num">{fmt(o.ece, 3)}</td><td class="num">{o.wrong_at_95 == null ? "—" : `${pct(o.wrong_at_95)}%`}</td><td class="num">{o.wrong_at_99 == null ? "—" : `${pct(o.wrong_at_99)}%`}</td><td class="num">{fmt(o.auroc, 3)}</td></tr>
                ); })}
              </Fragment>
            ); })}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="note">Luna's accuracy here is from the probe, a separate run at OpenAI's default sampling; the scored benchmark's figures ({pct(overall(LUNA, OWA).accuracy)}% and {pct(overall(LUNA, CWA).accuracy)}%) come from the earlier run. In {int(totalNotArgmax)} of {int(totalResponses)} responses ({listOf(taskOrder.map((s) => `${pt(s).returned_not_most_probable} ${TASK_INFO[s].short.toLowerCase()}`))}), the answer returned was not Luna's most probable listed option: that is sampling at the default temperature.</p>

      <h3>Separating right from wrong, by proof depth (AUROC)</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="cell-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Task</th><th scope="col">Depth</th><th scope="col" class="num">GPT-6 Luna</th>{others(OWA).filter((e) => pt(OWA).other_engines_by_depth[e]).map((e) => <th scope="col" class="num">{ENGINE_LABEL(e)}</th>)}</tr></thead>
          <tbody>{taskOrder.map((s) => Object.entries(pt(s).by_depth).map(([d, v], k) => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{k === 0 ? TASK_INFO[s].short : ""}</th><td>{d}</td><td class="num strong">{fmt(v.auroc, 3)}</td>
              {others(OWA).filter((e) => pt(OWA).other_engines_by_depth[e]).map((e) => { const x = (pt(s).other_engines_by_depth[e] || {})[d]; return <td class="num">{x && x.auroc != null ? fmt(x.auroc, 3) : "—"}</td>; })}</tr>
          )))}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>At proof depth 5, Luna's AUROC is {fmt(pt(OWA).by_depth["5"].auroc, 3)} on the open-world task and {fmt(pt(CWA).by_depth["5"].auroc, 3)} on the closed-world task: its probability says next to nothing about whether a deep answer is right.{pt(OWA).other_engines_by_depth.jev && ` Jev's is ${fmt(pt(OWA).other_engines_by_depth.jev["5"].auroc, 3)} and ${fmt(pt(CWA).other_engines_by_depth.jev["5"].auroc, 3)}.`} Jev's figures by depth are computed by this site from its saved probabilities, the same way as the analysis computes Luna's.</p>

      </div>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" aria-labelledby="mean-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="mean-h">What the probe means for a chat-endpoint decision model</h2>
    <p class="lede">A confidence is useful for routing: act on the sure answers, review the unsure ones. On these problems, Luna's stated confidence cannot do that without help.</p>
    <p><strong>Overconfidence can be calibrated.</strong> If a model's 99% answers are right {P && top(OWA) ? `${pct(top(OWA).accuracy)}%` : "less often"} of the time, you can learn that from labelled data and relabel them. That fixes what the number says, as long as higher stated probability still means more likely right.</p>
    <p><strong>Calibration cannot add information.</strong> AUROC measures only the ordering: whether right answers tend to get higher probability than wrong ones. Any recalibration that keeps that order leaves AUROC unchanged. Where AUROC is near 0.5, as Luna's is at depth 5, right and wrong answers get the same spread of probabilities, so no remapping of that probability can pick out which deep answers to trust. You would need a different signal.</p>
    <p>So a chat-endpoint decision model may be accurate enough on shallow problems, but on decisions that need several steps of reasoning, its own probability is not a usable confidence. Measure this on your own decisions before relying on it. The Decisions API's own confidence is reported above.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" aria-labelledby="ctx-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="ctx-h">Context: overconfidence and access to log-probabilities</h2>
    <ul class="how-list">
      <li><strong>Published evidence.</strong> OpenAI's <a href={S.gpt4.url}>{S.gpt4.label}</a> found the pre-trained model well calibrated, and that post-training reduced calibration markedly (its Figure 8).</li>
      <li><strong>In our experience</strong>, where OpenAI models do expose log-probabilities, they tend to be too overconfident to calibrate into a useful confidence.</li>
      <li><strong>Our suspicion, with no proof.</strong> Researchers recovered the final layer of production OpenAI models from API log-probabilities and logit bias, and OpenAI changed its API in response (<a href={S.carlini.url}>{S.carlini.label}</a>; see also <a href={S.finlayson.url}>{S.finlayson.label}</a>). OpenAI also chose not to show o1's raw chain of thought, citing user experience, competitive advantage and chain-of-thought monitoring (<a href={S.o1.url}>{S.o1.label}</a>). That may be why log-probabilities are refused with reasoning on, but none of this shows why.</li>
      <li><strong>Exposed, here.</strong> With reasoning off, Luna's probabilities were exposed almost completely. The problem this probe shows is overconfidence, not withheld probabilities.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section sec-prose" aria-labelledby="lim-h">
    <h2 class="display" id="lim-h">Limits</h2>
    <ul class="how-list">
      <li>One dataset, and a synthetic one: ProofWriter's templated reasoning problems, not your decisions.</li>
      <li>The probe covers the chat endpoint only; the Decisions API returns its own probabilities and confidence, reported above.</li>
      <li>The probe used OpenAI's default sampling, as the benchmark did, so a few answers were not Luna's most probable option.</li>
      <li>The probe is outside the scored benchmark: it changes no number elsewhere on this site. <a href={urls.page("repeatability")}>Calibration for every model</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

